1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
What if you actually fell into Jupiter?

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,200
In 1995,

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,100
NASA dropped a spacecraft into Jupiter.

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:10,180
It survived the entry.

5
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:13,300
It survived winds faster than some of Earth's

6
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:14,680
strongest hurricanes.

7
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:17,540
And it kept falling into a planet with no

8
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:18,440
ground.

9
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:20,500
Then, fifty eight minutes later,

10
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,080
its scientific transmission ended.

11
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:24,920
But here's the disturbing

12
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:27,320
part. The probe had barely scratched the

13
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:30,300
surface of Jupiter's enormous interior.

14
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:33,180
So what would happen if you kept falling?

15
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:36,740
Would you eventually hit something solid?

16
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,380
Would you sink into an ocean?

17
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,400
Or could you actually reach the center of

18
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:45,220
the largest planet in our solar system?

19
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,200
To find out, we're going to follow the real

20
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,080
spacecraft as far as it survived,

21
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,480
and then go somewhere no spacecraft has

22
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:54,700
ever reached.

23
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:57,320
But first, there's something about Jupiter

24
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,640
that makes this journey completely different

25
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:01,620
from falling toward Earth.

26
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:03,640
There's nowhere to land.

27
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:07,240
From space, Jupiter looks almost peaceful.

28
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:10,260
Beautiful orange and white stripes wrap

29
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:12,040
around the entire planet.

30
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:14,500
and that enormous red oval.

31
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,440
That's the great red spot,

32
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:20,180
a storm that has existed for centuries.

33
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:23,160
But those stripes aren't part of the ground,

34
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,920
They are clouds,

35
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:26,380
and they are moving.

36
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:29,320
Some of Jupiter's winds race at hundreds

37
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:31,320
of kilometers per hour.

38
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:34,040
The planet is so enormous that more than

39
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,360
thirteen hundred Earths could fit inside

40
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:37,500
its volume.

41
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:40,720
Yet somehow it completes a rotation in less

42
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:42,260
than ten hours.

43
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,000
That rapid rotation

44
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,040
helps create the powerful jet streams responsible

45
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,300
for its famous striped appearance.

46
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:51,940
But here's where things become unsettling.

47
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:56,080
NASA Juno spacecraft discovered that Jupiter's

48
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:59,760
major atmospheric winds extend roughly three

49
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:02,560
thousand kilometers below the visible cloud

50
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:03,660
tops,

51
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:05,440
3,000 kilometers.

52
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:09,260
Imagine a storm system extending deep into

53
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:10,460
a planet,

54
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:13,360
and you're about to fall straight into it.

55
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:16,240
But your first, real obstacle isn't the

56
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:17,140
wind.

57
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:18,740
It's your speed.

58
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:21,440
You're approaching Jupiter at approximately

59
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:23,680
one hundred seventy thousand kilometers

60
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:26,560
per hour, similar to the entry speed of

61
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:28,740
NASA Galileo probe,

62
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:30,840
Then you hit the upper atmosphere.

63
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:33,720
And suddenly everything changes.

64
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:36,820
The gas in front of your spacecraft is compressed

65
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:39,320
and heated to extreme temperatures.

66
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:42,980
Your heat shield begins absorbing an incredible

67
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:44,440
amount of energy.

68
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:47,380
Outside the darkness turns into a brilliant

69
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:48,280
glow.

70
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:50,600
Then the deceleration hits.

71
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:52,220
During Galileo's

72
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:55,260
real entry, the probe experienced a peak

73
00:02:55,260 --> 00:02:57,740
deceleration of approximately two hundred

74
00:02:57,740 --> 00:03:00,540
twenty eight times Earth's gravity,

75
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:02,640
two hundred and twenty eight.

76
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:05,280
A human couldn't survive that.

77
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:07,460
Not in an ordinary spacecraft.

78
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,340
Not with a normal space suit.

79
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:13,380
And definitely not by simply holding on.

80
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:14,660
But Galileo

81
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,720
was a specially designed robotic probe.

82
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:20,500
It survived the worst of the entry,

83
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:22,660
deployed its parachute,

84
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:24,900
and began transmitting information from

85
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:26,360
inside Jupiter.

86
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:30,260
Now imagine you have a fictional capsule

87
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:33,080
capable of protecting you from those forces.

88
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,000
The shaking stops.

89
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:36,840
The glow fades.

90
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,600
And for the first time,

91
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:40,620
you can see where you've arrived.

92
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:42,860
You're surrounded by clouds.

93
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:44,860
Above you, clouds.

94
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:46,780
Beside you, clouds.

95
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:48,720
Below you.

96
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:50,320
More clouds.

97
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:52,560
And something is missing.

98
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:54,000
A horizon.

99
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:57,020
On earth you can usually tell where the

100
00:03:57,020 --> 00:03:59,440
sky ends and the ground begins.

101
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:02,580
But inside Jupiter there is no familiar

102
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:05,000
landscape waiting beneath the atmosphere.

103
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,000
You check your instruments.

104
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:09,700
The surrounding pressure is approximately

105
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:10,980
one bar,

106
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:13,220
That's close to the atmospheric pressure

107
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:14,980
at earth's sea level.

108
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:17,720
For a moment that sounds almost safe.

109
00:04:17,860 --> 00:04:19,800
Until you see the temperature.

110
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,920
Minus one hundred eight degrees Celsius.

111
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:24,600
And the atmosphere.

112
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:27,880
Mostly hydrogen and helium.

113
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:31,080
There's no breathable oxygen supply.

114
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:33,620
So even though the pressure sounds familiar,

115
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:35,960
stepping outside would be fatal.

116
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:39,620
But something strange happens as you continue

117
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:40,520
descending.

118
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:42,920
The temperature starts rising.

119
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:45,380
You're falling deeper into Jupiter,

120
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:48,040
and the environment is becoming warmer.

121
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:49,220
Eventually,

122
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:51,380
you could pass through regions where the

123
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:53,780
temperature is comparable to a warm day

124
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:54,920
on Earth.

125
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:56,580
Sounds like an improvement,

126
00:04:56,580 --> 00:04:57,480
right?

127
00:04:57,860 --> 00:04:59,600
Except there's a catch.

128
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,240
The pressure is increasing too.

129
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:04,000
And unlike temperature,

130
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,580
pressure doesn't care whether you're comfortable.

131
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:09,280
It keeps rising as the atmosphere becomes

132
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:10,180
denser.

133
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:12,080
five bars

134
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:13,840
ten bars

135
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:15,360
fifteen

136
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:18,100
Now imagine looking out of your spacecraft.

137
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:21,040
You might expect to see an enormous ocean

138
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:21,940
approaching.

139
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:24,920
Because surely at some point all this gas

140
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:27,640
must end. But it doesn't end the way you

141
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:28,540
expect.

142
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:30,680
And before we reach that mystery,

143
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:32,840
something happens that scientists

144
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,060
actually experienced.

145
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,080
Galileo

146
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:38,640
discovers that Jupiter isn't behaving the

147
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:40,020
way they predicted.

148
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:42,160
As the probe descends,

149
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:44,160
its instruments measure the surrounding

150
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:45,060
atmosphere.

151
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:48,380
And one result is particularly surprising.

152
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,500
There's less water vapor than expected.

153
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:54,360
That matters because water abundance can

154
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:57,380
reveal clues about how Jupiter formed,

155
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:00,600
So did scientists misunderstand the planet.

156
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:02,520
Not necessarily.

157
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:04,100
Galileo

158
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:07,060
had entered an unusually dry atmospheric

159
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:09,620
region, It was like trying to understand

160
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:12,820
Earth's entire climate by dropping a weather

161
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,760
station into one desert.

162
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:17,460
One location couldn't represent the whole

163
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,360
planet,

164
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:20,700
But Galileo kept falling.

165
00:06:20,920 --> 00:06:22,780
The temperature climbed.

166
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,080
The pressure passed twenty times Earth's

167
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:26,620
sea level pressure.

168
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:28,940
And the probe continued transmitting.

169
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:31,680
until the signal was lost.

170
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:34,140
Its scientific data transmission

171
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:37,040
lasted approximately fifty eight minutes.

172
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:39,900
Its transmitter failed shortly afterward,

173
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:42,380
and the probe was ultimately destroyed by

174
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:45,680
Jupiter's increasingly extreme environment.

175
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,480
And here's what makes that so extraordinary.

176
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:51,340
At the last measured depth,

177
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:54,140
Galileo had reached only a tiny fraction

178
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:57,640
of the way into Jupiter's enormous interior,

179
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:00,200
Humanity had sent a spacecraft into the

180
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:03,560
largest planet in our solar system and barely

181
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:06,700
begun exploring what lay beneath its clouds.

182
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:09,200
So let's continue.

183
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:11,980
Not with real technology,

184
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:14,380
because no spacecraft we've built could

185
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:16,540
survive what comes next,

186
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:18,220
but with an imaginary,

187
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:19,260
indestructible

188
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:22,460
probe that allows us to explore what scientific

189
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:23,920
models predict.

190
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:26,980
As you descend, the hydrogen around you

191
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:29,080
becomes increasingly compressed.

192
00:07:29,380 --> 00:07:31,160
The density rises.

193
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:33,480
The pressure becomes enormous.

194
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:36,740
And gradually, the material begins behaving

195
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:39,540
less like an ordinary gas and more like

196
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:41,000
a dense fluid.

197
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:43,380
But here's the weird part.

198
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:45,800
You never splash into an ocean.

199
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:48,280
There's no enormous wave.

200
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:50,280
No clear water line.

201
00:07:50,340 --> 00:07:52,740
No sudden moment when your spacecraft

202
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:55,180
enters a completely separate sea.

203
00:07:55,180 --> 00:07:58,300
Instead, the hydrogen rich material changes

204
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,260
its properties as pressure and temperature

205
00:08:01,260 --> 00:08:02,160
increase.

206
00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:04,940
You are moving into the deep fluid interior

207
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:06,080
of Jupiter.

208
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:09,680
And now we reach something even stranger.

209
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:11,900
Because much farther down,

210
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:14,700
hydrogen is expected to transform into a

211
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:17,440
state that sounds like science fiction.

212
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:19,140
Metallic hydrogen.

213
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:22,580
Now, don't imagine enormous silver rocks.

214
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:26,180
Or a shiny metal ocean like liquid mercury.

215
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,980
That's not what scientists mean.

216
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:30,800
Under extreme pressure,

217
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:33,780
hydrogen can become electrically conductive.

218
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:36,960
Its electrons can move in ways that allow

219
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:39,140
electric currents to flow.

220
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:41,740
And scientists believe enormous regions

221
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:45,340
of Jupiter's interior contain liquid metallic

222
00:08:45,340 --> 00:08:46,240
hydrogen.

223
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:48,640
But why does that matter?

224
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,360
because it helps solve a mystery from the

225
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:53,300
very beginning of your journey.

226
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:57,120
Remember Jupiter's powerful magnetic field,

227
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:59,760
the one responsible for trapping dangerous

228
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:00,720
radiation

229
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:02,440
around the planet,

230
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:04,600
Deep inside Jupiter,

231
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:06,840
the movement of electrically conducting

232
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:10,360
fluid helps generate that magnetic field.

233
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:12,060
Think about that.

234
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:14,940
The invisible danger surrounding Jupiter

235
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:18,220
is connected to the movement of exotic material

236
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:20,380
buried deep inside it.

237
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:22,780
Something you couldn't see from space was

238
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:25,420
already revealing what was happening thousands

239
00:09:25,420 --> 00:09:27,760
of kilometers beneath the clouds.

240
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:31,100
But we still haven't reached the center.

241
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:33,880
And that's where Jupiter delivers its biggest

242
00:09:33,880 --> 00:09:34,780
surprise.

243
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:38,040
For years, scientists modeled Jupiter as

244
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:39,800
an enormous hydrogen-rich

245
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:42,520
planet, surrounding a relatively compact

246
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:44,460
core of heavier material.

247
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:47,180
something like a giant ball with a smaller,

248
00:09:47,180 --> 00:09:48,640
denser center.

249
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:51,660
But NASA Juno spacecraft complicated that

250
00:09:51,660 --> 00:09:54,860
picture. By measuring tiny variations in

251
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:57,260
Jupiter's gravitational field,

252
00:09:57,260 --> 00:09:59,900
scientists found evidence supporting a much

253
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:02,240
more diffused central region.

254
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:03,920
A fuzzy core.

255
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:06,460
Instead of all the heavier elements being

256
00:10:06,460 --> 00:10:10,060
concentrated in one sharply defined ball,

257
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:12,220
they may be spread through a broad region

258
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:14,260
of the planet's interior,

259
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:16,980
And scientists still don't know exactly

260
00:10:16,980 --> 00:10:18,920
how that structure developed.

261
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:22,740
Some models involve processes during Jupiter's

262
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:23,640
formation.

263
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:26,420
Others explore whether a giant collision

264
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:28,740
early in its history could have altered

265
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:30,360
the planet's interior.

266
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:33,180
But no spacecraft has ever reached that

267
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:34,080
region.

268
00:10:34,140 --> 00:10:36,540
No camera has photographed it.

269
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:39,740
And nobody can tell you exactly what Jupiter's

270
00:10:39,740 --> 00:10:41,280
center looks like.

271
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:44,060
Which brings us back to the question we

272
00:10:44,060 --> 00:10:46,700
started with. If you actually fell into

273
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:49,760
Jupiter, would you eventually land?

274
00:10:50,140 --> 00:10:51,040
No.

275
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:53,880
You wouldn't reach a normal solid surface

276
00:10:53,880 --> 00:10:55,900
like the ground on Earth.

277
00:10:55,960 --> 00:10:57,800
With real technology,

278
00:10:57,800 --> 00:11:00,120
your spacecraft would be destroyed long

279
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:02,860
before reaching Jupiter's deepest interior.

280
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:06,240
And even with our imaginary indestructible

281
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:08,960
probe, you wouldn't find the simple rocky

282
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:11,040
landscape you might expect.

283
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:13,680
Instead, you would travel through an atmosphere

284
00:11:13,680 --> 00:11:16,400
that gradually becomes an incredibly dense

285
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:17,440
fluid,

286
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:20,000
into regions where hydrogen behaves like

287
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,640
a metal, and toward a central structure

288
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:25,620
that science is still trying to understand.

289
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:29,560
And here's the most surprising part of all.

290
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:32,440
From Earth, Jupiter looks like a giant striped

291
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:35,240
ball. But those beautiful stripes are only

292
00:11:35,240 --> 00:11:36,860
its visible clouds.

293
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:40,380
Almost everything that makes Jupiter extraordinary

294
00:11:40,380 --> 00:11:42,380
is hidden beneath them.

295
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:44,780
We can photograph its storms,

296
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:46,860
measure its gravity,

297
00:11:46,860 --> 00:11:49,060
study its magnetic field,

298
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:52,020
and predict the exotic materials deep inside

299
00:11:52,020 --> 00:11:54,820
it. But we've never directly explored the

300
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:57,800
overwhelming majority of its interior.

301
00:11:58,180 --> 00:12:00,420
So the next time you see Jupiter shining

302
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:02,900
in the night sky, remember this,

303
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:05,620
You're not looking at a giant ball of gas

304
00:12:05,620 --> 00:12:08,020
with a secret landing spot.

305
00:12:08,020 --> 00:12:10,740
You're looking at a world so extreme that

306
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:13,780
even after sending spacecraft across hundreds

307
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:16,260
of millions of kilometers,

308
00:12:16,260 --> 00:12:19,100
we've barely begun to discover what's inside.

